          "type": "turn",
          "question": "My dad is 80, confused since this morning and has not passed water since yesterday. How urgent is this, and what would be looked for?",
          "answer": "**Call emergency services now, or take him to the nearest emergency department. Do not wait to see if this passes.**\n\nThe risk-check tool returned \"routine\" because none of its fixed rules matched that exact wording. That is not a clearance. The sepsis guidance I retrieved (Critical care and emergency medicine society guidance) lists confusion and \"not passing urine for many hours\" as signs that warrant emergency assessment. Your father has both, and the urine gap is about a day. I am treating this as high urgency. Please act on that now.\n\nWhile you wait for help, keep him with someone, and tell the responders when the confusion started and when he last passed urine. Do not give him any medicine or change his medicines unless a clinician tells you to.\n\n**What clinicians would look for:** The passages I retrieved do not cover urinary retention, kidney injury, dehydration, electrolyte problems, stroke, or medication effects in any detail, so I cannot explain those from my sources. Clinicians usually check for them early, along with infection. The sources do support these points:\n- **Infection and sepsis:** Confusion in an older adult can be the main sign of a urinary infection. The sepsis guidance treats confusion with reduced urine as an emergency in someone with a suspected infection.\n- **Blood sugar:** If he has diabetes, very high or very low blood sugar can cause confusion and drowsiness, and both need immediate medical attention (Type 2 diabetes reference).\n- **Thyroid:** Severe underactive thyroid with confusion or drowsiness is a rare emergency (Hypothyroidism reference).\n\nTypical first steps in an emergency department are a physical examination, blood and urine tests, and checks of blood pressure, heart rate, temperature and oxygen. Those details come from general practice, not from my sources, so treat them as an outline only.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
